<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Babylonian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as First Dynasty of Babylon, First Dynasty, Old Babylonian Kingdom, First Babylonian Dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Old Babylonian Empire, or First Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, was an Amorite state in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> that grew to dominate the region under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> in the eighteenth century BCE. It is known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> and the elevation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> as supreme god. It was ended around 1595 BCE by the Hittite raid on Babylon under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mursili-i">Mursili I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire.jpg" alt="Old Babylonian Empire" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babylone_1.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Old Babylonian Empire, also called the First Babylonian Empire, comes after the end of Sumerian power with the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> and the Isin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> period that followed. Its origins are hard to pinpoint, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> itself yields few intact archaeological materials owing to a high water table. The evidence that survives includes royal and votive inscriptions, literary texts and lists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/year-name">year-names</a>, while economic and legal documents are few.</p><p>Little is known of the kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumuabum">Sumuabum</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sin-muballit">Sin-muballit</a>, except that they were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadians</a> and added little to the territory. Sumuabum conquered Dilbat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and began a wall around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, which his successor Sumualailum completed. Sin-muballit defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a>, which protected Babylon from further invasion, and passed the throne to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> from 1792 to 1750 BCE. At first his realm consisted of a few towns near Babylon: Dilbat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borsippa">Borsippa</a>, and Babylon was only one of several important powers beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>. By 1762 BCE he had captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, inheriting its trade routes, and his armies then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>. In 1761 BCE Babylon gained control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, giving it virtually all the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> that had been under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>. In his thirtieth year he conquered Larsa from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a> and gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and Isin.</p><p>The archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, are a main source for the diplomacy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s reign. They survived because Hammurabi burned the palace down, which buried the tablets. His successors from Samsuiluna to Samsuditana left few records. Samsuiluna defeated Rim-Sîn II but lost major parts of the conquered land and kept real authority only over the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>. The dynasty came to an end as the empire lost territory and money, and attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> expanding out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> led to the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. The Kassite period followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> comes from this dynasty and is among the oldest written laws and the best known artifacts of the ancient world. It is written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> on a diorite stele 2.25 metres high. At the top the king receives his kingship from the sun god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, and below is the collection of laws. The text says that the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> to provide just ways for the people of the land, and it presents the laws as the divine role given to him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> came to dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> for over a thousand years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Old Babylonian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Mursili I raided Babylon around 1595 BCE and ended the <b>First Babylonian Dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hammurabi, sixth king of the Amorite <b>First Dynasty of Babylon</b>, ruled from around 1792 to around 1750 BCE (middle chronology).</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A new capital, Memphis, was founded where the Nile valley meets the Delta, and the <b>First Dynasty</b> began, succeeding the Proto-kingdom of Thinis.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Abydos remained the burial ground of the <b>First Dynasty</b> kings and later became the great cult centre of Osiris.</blockquote><a href="/state/proto-kingdom-of-thinis">Proto-kingdom of Thinis</a></li></ul></section>
